# DOCUMENTATION, NOT A WORKFLOW.
#
# The filename ends in .disabled on purpose: Gitea only reads *.yml and *.yaml
# in this directory, so this file cannot be triggered. It describes how the CI
# image is built, and why that is done by hand.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT RUN HERE: a job on this runner gets the container docker:cli,
# which talks to the host's Docker. `docker build` reads its context on the
# client side, so the context has to exist *inside the job container* — and the
# one thing the image needs that the repository cannot carry is Quartus 18.1:
# 4 GB of licensed files, staged on the NAS as a 1.8 GB archive. From the job
# container that path does not exist, and a bind mount would not help either,
# for the same reason the firmware jobs no longer use one (see build.yml).
#
# So the image is built over SSH on the NAS. It takes 30 to 45 minutes cold and
# is needed again only when build/Dockerfile.ci changes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MANUAL PROCEDURE, as used on 2026-09-18
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Staging directory on the NAS (Quartus archive stays there between builds):
#
#     /share/Container/1541ultimate-ci/context/
#         Dockerfile.ci          copy of build/Dockerfile.ci
#         quartus-18.1.tar.gz    nios2eds + quartus, 1.8 GB compressed
#
# 1. Build the archive on the Mac and stream it over, once, or again after a
#    Quartus update. hls and ip are left out: 4.0 GB instead of 6.7.
#
#        cd ~/quartus-18.1
#        tar -cf - --exclude='._*' nios2eds quartus | gzip -1 \
#          | ssh admin@iphnnas 'cat > /share/Container/1541ultimate-ci/context/quartus-18.1.tar.gz'
#
# 2. Copy the Dockerfile of the commit you want to build:
#
#        scp build/Dockerfile.ci \
#            admin@iphnnas:/share/Container/1541ultimate-ci/context/Dockerfile.ci
#
# 3. Build, detached. QNAP has no nohup, but it does have setsid — without it
#    the build dies with the SSH session. Docker lives outside PATH there:
#
#        ssh admin@iphnnas '
#          D=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/.qpkg/container-station/bin/docker
#          cd /share/Container/1541ultimate-ci/context
#          setsid "$D" build -t u64-build-env:ci -t u64-build-env:latest \
#            -f Dockerfile.ci . > build.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &'
#
# 4. Watch build.log, then prove the image contains what its tag claims —
#    a missing toolchain should surface here, not forty minutes into a
#    firmware job:
#
#        ssh admin@iphnnas '
#          D=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/.qpkg/container-station/bin/docker
#          $D run --rm u64-build-env:ci bash -c "
#            nios2-elf-gcc --version | head -1
#            riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc --version | head -1
#            test -f /opt/altera_lite/18.1/quartus/bin/quartus_cpf
#            test -d /opt/esp/esp-idf/components/esp_rom/esp32s3
#            test -f /opt/esp/esp-idf/components/heap/tlsf/tlsf.c"'
#
# No registry, no push: the firmware jobs run on the same Docker, so a locally
# tagged image is all they need. Pushing would also require the daemon to
# accept a plain-HTTP registry. Result on 2026-09-18: 13 GB, and the failed
# attempts are kept next to build.log as build-failed-*.log.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT A RUNNABLE VERSION WOULD LOOK LIKE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept for the day the staging problem is solved — by a runner whose workspace
# is a host path, or by hosting the Quartus archive somewhere the job can fetch
# it from.
#
# name: build-image
#
# on:
#   workflow_dispatch:
#
